    <h1>About Fran Hopper</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="copy">
    <p><strong>Frances R. Deitrick Hopper</strong> (July 13, 1922 – November 29, 2017) was an American comic-book artist who worked during the Golden Age of Comic Books. She was one of the earliest women to work professionally in the American comic book industry.</p>

    <h2>Early Life and Career</h2>
    <p>Born in Maryland, Fran Hopper studied at the Pratt Institute before entering the comics field. During World War II, as many male comic artists were drafted into military service, publishers opened their doors to female creators.</p>
    <p>In 1943, she was hired by the S. M. "Jerry" Iger Studio, one of the era's "packaging studios" that produced outsourced comics for publishers. Editor Ruth Roche recruited her alongside other talented women including Ruth Atkinson, Lily Renée, and Marcia Snyder.</p>

    <h2>Notable Works</h2>
    <p>Hopper worked primarily for Fiction House, contributing to several beloved features:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Gale Allen and the Girl Squadron</strong> – A science-fiction adventure featuring a female space captain leading an all-woman battalion (Planet Comics #28–40, 1944–1945)</li>
      <li><strong>Mysta of the Moon</strong> – A female super-scientist defending Earth from cosmic threats (Planet Comics #37–49, 1945–1947)</li>
      <li><strong>Jane Martin</strong> – An espionage series (Wings Comics, 1946–1947)</li>
      <li><strong>Camilla</strong> – A jungle adventure feature (Jungle Comics, 1945–1947)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Comic historian Trina Robbins noted that Hopper's "art for 'Mysta of the Moon' was often stunning."</p>

    <h2>Historical Significance</h2>
    <p>Fran Hopper's historical significance lies in her role as a pioneer for women in the comic book industry. Her work featured strong female protagonists at a time when such portrayals were rare—Gale Allen commanded an all-woman space battalion, and Mysta was a brilliant scientist whose knowledge exceeded that of any man.</p>
    <p>In 2000, she was nominated to the Friends of Lulu Hall of Fame. Comic artist and historian Trina Robbins tracked her down, leading to renewed interest in her career. She was featured in Robbins' anthology <em>Babes In Arms: Women in the Comics During World War Two</em> (2017).</p>

    <h2>Public Domain Status</h2>
    <p>Fran Hopper's comic work is in the public domain. Fiction House's comics from the 1940s entered the public domain because the company ceased operations in 1954 and did not renew copyrights on most of its catalog under the 1909 Copyright Act.</p>
    <p>The comics featured on this site are freely available from archives such as <a href="https://comicbookplus.com/">Comic Book Plus</a> and the <a href="https://digitalcomicmuseum.com/">Digital Comic Museum</a>.</p>

    <h2>About This Site</h2>
    <p>This demonstration site showcases how to build an MCP-enabled webcomic archive. The framework is open-source under the MIT license. The sample content—Fran Hopper's Golden Age comics—is public domain and included to demonstrate the system's capabilities.</p>
    <p>You can replace this sample content with your own comics by following the tutorial in the <code>docs/</code> folder.</p>
